# UI primitive hygiene — close-out audit (chain the modularizer)

## Pyramid Layer

- Layer: L1 core rule.

## Use This When

- Keep this rule active for any work that adds or changes USER-FACING UI — renders
  JSX/markup, styles a component, or builds a view / dialog / toolbar / form / menu.

## Stop Here If

- Stop after the close-out + variant-extraction obligations are clear; this is a leaf rule.

## Descend To

- The WHAT (which primitive, how to compose): `shadcn-patterns-required.mdc` +
  `shadcn-patterns-react-ui.mdc` + the shadcn skill (`/.agents/skills/shadcn/SKILL.md`).
- The WHO (execution): the **`refactoring-modularizer`** agent
  (`.claude/agents/refactoring-modularizer.local.md`).

## Why this rule

A feature pass naturally accretes raw HTML controls (`<button>`, `<input>`, `<select>`, …)
and per-call-site `className` blobs — the fastest way to ship pixels. Left alone they drift
from the design system: a raw `<button>` skips the `@workspace/ui` `Button`'s a11y +
variants, and the same className cluster copy-pasted across call-sites is one shape that was
NOT standardised (an un-named de-facto variant). `shadcn-patterns-required` says *use the
primitive*; this rule adds the **close-out that enforces it**, exactly the way
`seed-dictionary-coverage` chains the `seed-curator`. This rule was added after a refactor
where raw `<button>`s and repeated `border-border / hover:bg-transparent / h-auto p-0`
neutralization blobs had accreted and were lifted into `Button` `crumb`/`segmented`/`pill`
variants.

## Required behavior

1. **Feature work ends with a primitive-hygiene audit.** When a change adds or alters UI,
   BEFORE reporting done, get the UI primitive-clean: every raw HTML control that a
   `@workspace/ui` primitive already covers (Button, Input, Select, Dialog, Checkbox, …)
   routes through that primitive (the `CardTile`-style complex surfaces that already use a
   primitive are fine). Do this as the **close-out of the feature**, by **chaining the
   `refactoring-modularizer` agent** when you can spawn sub-agents, or INLINE following its
   methodology when you cannot — alongside the `seed-curator` close-out, not instead of it.

2. **Recurring className clusters become shared variants.** When the SAME override cluster
   repeats across ≥2 call-sites (a chip, a breadcrumb crumb, a segmented toggle, …), lift it
   into a reusable, semantic, token-only variant/size on the shared primitive (e.g. a
   `Button` cva variant) rather than copy-pasting the blob. The variant name reads as generic
   UI vocabulary, never a feature name. Behaviour + rendered look MUST be unchanged — verify
   with the repo gates + the relevant e2e. A genuinely one-off control stays a plain
   variant/size; do not force-fit it into a shape variant.

3. **Exemptions.** A change with no JSX/markup (data layer, migration, pure logic) is exempt
   — say so. Genuinely one-off, non-recurring styling is exempt from #2.

## Rationale

One primitive + a small set of semantic variants means the UI is consistent, accessible, and
DRY by construction — and a className blob never silently becomes the de-facto Nth copy of an
un-named variant. Drift (a raw control where a primitive exists; a repeated className cluster
that should be a variant) is what this rule prevents.
